# The NMEA port of an LTE modem's GNSS, for tslocationd (docs/location.md, "Receivers").
#
# ModemManager's own rules (77-mm-*-port-types.rules) mark a modem's NMEA port with
# ID_MM_PORT_TYPE_GPS, so this rule runs after them. It gives the port a name next to the
# kernel's (ttyUSB1 -> ttyUSBgnss1; not gnss%n, which is the kernel's GNSS subsystem) and
# adds it to gpsd through tsos-gpsd-add@.service, which waits for gpsd.socket. Debian's
# gpsdctl@.service would only do that with USBAUTO="true", which /etc/default/gpsd keeps off
# so gpsd does not open every USB serial port.
#
# tslocationd switches the GNSS on and off through ModemManager's gps-unmanaged source; while
# it is off, gpsd sees a silent device. gpsd drops the device by itself when it is unplugged.
# After changing this file on a running system: udevadm control --reload; udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=tty
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ENV{ID_MM_PORT_TYPE_GPS}=="1", SYMLINK+="ttyUSBgnss%n", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="tsos-gpsd-add@%k.service"
